// =============================================================================
// SAM Group Platform — sam_platform
//
// A literal translation of docs/DATA_MODEL.md §1: 23 entities plus three join
// tables — BlogPost/BlogTag, Product/Segment, and the ordered
// Segment/ProductType. Not a redesign.
//
// Segment, ProductType and the two catalog joins arrive from ADR-007 (Product
// Taxonomy v2) and are additive by decision: Category remains the Product
// Family axis, Product.categoryId is untouched, and no column is dropped or
// rewritten. Eight Segment slugs are approved by ADR-008. Their INITIAL approved
// reference-data population mechanism is the dedicated, explicitly invoked
// catalog seed (prisma/seed-catalog.ts) — not a migration, and not
// `prisma db seed`, which remains locale-only. The per-Segment Product Type
// lists remain unapproved, as does every ProductType name and slug.
//
// Boundaries this file must never cross:
//   - It models sam_platform only. Payload owns sam_cms and is never modelled
//     here, in any form (ADR-002).
//   - References that cross into sam_cms are soft string keys, never foreign
//     keys: JobApplication.jobOpeningKey, DownloadRequest.documentKey.
//   - ADR-012 adds a second set of database-enforced objects, listed with the
//     ADR-011 ones below: users_credential_revocation_guard (BEFORE UPDATE
//     ROW) advances users.credentials_revoked_at on active → disabled and
//     refuses to move it backwards, and users_revoke_sessions_on_disable
//     (AFTER UPDATE STATEMENT) revokes that user's live auth_sessions in the
//     same transaction. Disable is revocation, not suspension, and the
//     invariant does not depend on a status-management endpoint that does not
//     exist yet.
//   - AuthSession is an application-session entity added by ADR-012, not a
//     Payload one: Payload keeps its own users_sessions in sam_cms and the
//     two are unrelated (ADR-006).
//   - Future-phase entities (DATA_MODEL.md §2 — Order, Quote, Lead, Workflow*,
//     Notification, ExternalSyncMap) are deliberately absent until their phase
//     starts.
//   - RAG has no presence here. Its vector store is a separate, isolated
//     database (docs/ai/RAG_ARCHITECTURE.md §0).
//
// Conventions:
//   - Physical tables and columns are snake_case (CODING_STANDARDS.md); the
//     Prisma API stays PascalCase models / camelCase fields.
//   - PostgreSQL maps Prisma `String` to `text` already, so DATA_MODEL.md's
//     string/text distinction needs no annotation and no varchar limits are
//     imposed.
//   - PostgreSQL does not auto-index foreign keys, so every FK column carries
//     an explicit @@index.
//
// Three constraints cannot be expressed here and must be added by hand to the
// first migration (approved, pending the migration phase):
//   1. locales_single_default              — only one Locale.isDefault
//   2. redirects_from_path_global          — unique from_path where locale IS NULL
//   3. content_translations_unique_slug    — unique slug per (entity_type, locale)
//
// ADR-011 adds a second group of hand-written objects, in migration
// 20260814120000_add_product_slug_namespace_registry. Prisma models the
// ProductSlugClaim TABLE below; everything that MAINTAINS it is SQL this file
// cannot express, so it lives only in that migration and is listed here for the
// same reason the three above are — nothing else makes it visible.
//
//   Functions:
//     slug_key(text)                        — lower(normalize(value, NFC))
//     product_slug_reserved()               — the ADR-010 §4 reserved values
//     product_slug_sources()                — the full candidate set, all 3 tables
//     product_slug_owner_exists(...)        — INV-3 owner check
//     product_slug_owner_uses_key(...)      — the release recomputation
//     product_slug_claim(...)               — the one claim implementation
//     product_slug_release(...)             — the one guarded release
//     product_slug_release_owner(...)       — unconditional release on entity delete
//     product_slug_tg_* (9)                 — one trigger function per table/event
//
//   Triggers (statement-level AFTER, with transition tables), 9 in total:
//     product_slug_categories_{insert,update,delete}
//     product_slug_products_{insert,update,delete}
//     product_slug_translations_{insert,update,delete}
//
// `prisma migrate reset` drops all of it, exactly as it drops the three
// constraints above; `prisma migrate deploy` restores it.
//
// PRODUCT-DATA-2A adds a THIRD group of objects this file cannot express, across
// migrations 20260822120000_add_catalog_technical_data and
// 20260822140000_harden_catalog_technical_audit. The decision behind all of it is
// [ADR-014](../docs/ADR/ADR-014-catalog-technical-data-and-provenance.md), which
// also records the ONE thing the database deliberately does NOT enforce: how a
// row reaches review_status = approved. That transition belongs to the review
// service in PRODUCT-DATA-2B, and no generic update path may ever expose the
// column. Listed here for the same reason the two groups above are: nothing else
// makes them visible.
//
//   CHECK constraints:
//     specifications_value_shape             — numeric/pair columns must match
//                                              value_type, and min <= max
//     specifications_normalized_complete     — a typed row carries a property
//                                              key and a display value
//     product_claims_forbidden_approval      — LICENSED_BY and REFERENCE_ONLY
//                                              can never be APPROVED
//     product_claims_approved_by_named_body  — APPROVED_BY + APPROVED requires
//                                              a non-empty standard_body
//     technical_reviews_exactly_one_target   — a review has one subject, never
//                                              two and never none
//
//   Functions:
//     evidence_set_hash_lines(text[])        — the deterministic encoding
//     specification_evidence_set_hash(uuid)  — the reviewed-evidence fingerprint
//     product_claim_evidence_set_hash(uuid)  — the ProductClaim counterpart
//     source_facts_immutable()               — the trigger guard body
//
//   Trigger (BEFORE UPDATE ROW, ENABLE ALWAYS):
//     source_facts_immutable_guard           — BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE: a
//                                              recorded source fact is evidence
//                                              and can be neither rewritten nor
//                                              deleted. INSERT is untouched.
//
//   Views — the ONLY sanctioned public read surface for technical data:
//     v_specification_public                 — approved, live, allow-listed
//     v_product_claim_public                 — approved, live, allow-listed,
//                                              forbidden kinds excluded again
//   Both views are SELECT-only: INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/TRUNCATE are revoked from
//   PUBLIC and from the owner. A guard rail, not a privilege boundary — see
//   ADR-014 §8 for exactly what that does and does not buy.
//
// The views carry no review-status column and no provenance column, and they
// join nothing in the Technical Data Provenance section. That section is
// import/audit only and is categorically non-public.
// =============================================================================

// Prisma 7 no longer accepts `url` here — the connection string for Migrate
// lives in prisma.config.ts, and PrismaClient receives a driver adapter.
datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
}

generator client {
  provider            = "prisma-client"
  output              = "../apps/api/src/prisma/generated"
  moduleFormat        = "cjs"
  runtime             = "nodejs"
  importFileExtension = "ts"
}

// --- Enums -------------------------------------------------------------------
// Seven enums, for closed value sets only. Submission `status` fields are
// deliberately String: the business lifecycle is not yet defined.
// Physical labels are the lowercase strings the documentation specifies; the
// human-readable forms ("Request a Quote") are presentation strings and belong
// to the translation catalogs, not to a Postgres type.

enum UserRole {
  ADMIN           @map("admin")
  CONTENT_MANAGER @map("content_manager")
  SALES_EXPERT    @map("sales_expert")
  CUSTOMER        @map("customer")

  @@map("user_role")
}

// Account status, and deliberately only two values (ADR-012). `suspended`,
// `locked`, `pending` and a `deleted` soft-delete state are NOT members:
// nothing on this platform distinguishes them, and an unused enum label is a
// lifecycle nobody implemented. Deleting a row remains a real delete.
enum UserStatus {
  ACTIVE   @map("active")
  DISABLED @map("disabled")

  @@map("user_status")
}

enum InquiryType {
  PRODUCT_INQUIRY          @map("product_inquiry")
  REQUEST_A_QUOTE          @map("request_a_quote")
  CUSTOMIZED_SOLUTION      @map("customized_solution")
  EXPORT_AND_LOGISTICS     @map("export_and_logistics")
  DISTRIBUTION_PARTNERSHIP @map("distribution_partnership")
  GENERAL_INQUIRY          @map("general_inquiry")
  SAMPLE_REQUEST           @map("sample_request")

  @@map("inquiry_type")
}

enum NewsletterStatus {
  PENDING      @map("pending")
  CONFIRMED    @map("confirmed")
  UNSUBSCRIBED @map("unsubscribed")

  @@map("newsletter_status")
}

enum LocaleDirection {
  LTR @map("ltr")
  RTL @map("rtl")

  @@map("locale_direction")
}

enum TranslationStatus {
  MACHINE_DRAFT  @map("machine_draft")
  HUMAN_REVIEWED @map("human_reviewed")

  @@map("translation_status")
}

enum MediaType {
  IMAGE    @map("image")
  FILE     @map("file")
  VIDEO    @map("video")
  DOCUMENT @map("document")

  @@map("media_type")
}

// --- Technical Data (PRODUCT-DATA-2A) ----------------------------------------
// Closed vocabularies for Prisma-owned catalog technical data. Every one of them
// is INTERNAL until a row carrying it is APPROVED — the enums describe what may
// be recorded, never what may be published. Publication is decided by
// `review_status` and the two public views, not by membership of a type.

/// The grade vocabularies this catalog actually uses. NULL on a ProductGrade
/// means "not yet safely classified" and is a legitimate, expected state — it is
/// NOT a fourth system and must never be backfilled by guesswork.
///
/// `PRODUCT_SPECIFIC` is deliberately absent: a label belonging to no published
/// system is recorded verbatim in `ProductGrade.label` with a NULL system, which
/// says the same thing without inventing a classification.
enum GradeSystem {
  SAE    @map("sae")
  ISO_VG @map("iso_vg")
  NLGI   @map("nlgi")

  @@map("grade_system")
}

/// The SHAPE of a normalized technical value — what the numeric columns mean,
/// not what the property is. Enforced by the `specifications_value_shape` CHECK
/// added in the same migration, so an inconsistent combination cannot be stored.
///
///   POINT       one value            numericMin
///   RANGE       lower and upper      numericMin + numericMax, min <= max
///   MINIMUM     a floor              numericMin
///   MAXIMUM     a ceiling            numericMax
///   PAIR        two coupled values   pairFirst + pairSecond
///   TEXT        prose                no numeric columns
///   REPORT_ONLY the source states the property is reported, not limited
///   CODE        a classification code carried as text
enum SpecValueType {
  POINT       @map("point")
  RANGE       @map("range")
  MINIMUM     @map("minimum")
  MAXIMUM     @map("maximum")
  TEXT        @map("text")
  REPORT_ONLY @map("report_only")
  CODE        @map("code")
  PAIR        @map("pair")

  @@map("spec_value_type")
}

/// What kind of value a SpecProperty carries at all — a coarser axis than
/// `SpecValueType`, which describes one recorded value. Viscosity is NUMERIC and
/// may legitimately arrive as POINT, RANGE or MINIMUM; appearance is TEXTUAL.
enum SpecValueKind {
  NUMERIC @map("numeric")
  TEXTUAL @map("textual")
  CODED   @map("coded")

  @@map("spec_value_kind")
}

/// Whether a test method must accompany a value for that property to mean
/// anything. A viscosity without its ASTM method is not the same fact as one
/// with it.
enum MethodRequirement {
  REQUIRED       @map("required")
  OPTIONAL       @map("optional")
  NOT_APPLICABLE @map("not_applicable")

  @@map("method_requirement")
}

/// What a recorded number actually IS. This is the difference between a
/// marketing figure and a specification limit, and it is never inferred.
///
/// UNSPECIFIED is the honest default and the value every legacy row carries: the
/// source did not say. The public label for AVERAGE is "Average test results"
/// and belongs to the translation catalogs, not to this type.
enum ResultBasis {
  AVERAGE             @map("average")
  TYPICAL             @map("typical")
  SPECIFICATION_LIMIT @map("specification_limit")
  MEASURED            @map("measured")
  UNSPECIFIED         @map("unspecified")

  @@map("result_basis")
}

/// The publication gate. Exactly one value — APPROVED — lets a fact reach the
/// public views, and nothing reaches them by default: every row is born
/// SOURCE_RECORDED.
///
///   SOURCE_RECORDED  extracted from a source, never reviewed
///   NEEDS_REVIEW     queued for SAM technical review
///   APPROVED         SAM technical approval recorded — the ONLY public state
///   REJECTED         reviewed and refused
///   SUPERSEDED       replaced by a later fact; kept, never deleted
enum TechnicalReviewStatus {
  SOURCE_RECORDED @map("source_recorded")
  NEEDS_REVIEW    @map("needs_review")
  APPROVED        @map("approved")
  REJECTED        @map("rejected")
  SUPERSEDED      @map("superseded")

  @@map("technical_review_status")
}

/// What a reviewer DECIDED, as opposed to what state a row is IN.
/// `SOURCE_RECORDED` has no counterpart here by design: it is the state before
/// any human decision, so it can never be the outcome of one.
enum TechnicalReviewDecision {
  APPROVED     @map("approved")
  REJECTED     @map("rejected")
  NEEDS_REVIEW @map("needs_review")
  SUPERSEDED   @map("superseded")

  @@map("technical_review_decision")
}

/// The strength of a product claim, from weakest to strongest. The distinction
/// is legal, not stylistic: "formulated for" and "approved by" are different
/// statements, and conflating them is the specific failure this type prevents.
///
///   CLASSIFICATION_STATED  a bare API/ACEA class the source states
///   MEETS                  the source states the product meets a standard
///   SUITABLE_FOR           suitability, not conformance
///   RECOMMENDED_FOR        a recommendation
///   FORMULATED_FOR         an additive target level — NOT an approval
///   APPROVED_BY            a named body has approved it
///   LICENSED_BY            a named body has licensed it
///   REFERENCE_ONLY         recorded for internal reference; never publishable
enum ProductClaimKind {
  CLASSIFICATION_STATED @map("classification_stated")
  MEETS                 @map("meets")
  SUITABLE_FOR          @map("suitable_for")
  RECOMMENDED_FOR       @map("recommended_for")
  FORMULATED_FOR        @map("formulated_for")
  APPROVED_BY           @map("approved_by")
  LICENSED_BY           @map("licensed_by")
  REFERENCE_ONLY        @map("reference_only")

  @@map("product_claim_kind")
}

/// How a SourceDocument is addressed. A URL is one case among several, not the
/// model: the authoritative catalog workbook is an uploaded file with no URL at
/// all, and a schema that required one would have no way to cite it.
enum SourceLocatorType {
  URL           @map("url")
  UPLOADED_FILE @map("uploaded_file")

  @@map("source_locator_type")
}

/// How a SourceFact was got out of its document. Frozen because it decides how
/// far a fact is trusted: an OCR reading and a spreadsheet cell are not
/// equivalent evidence, and a reviewer must be able to tell them apart.
enum ExtractionMethod {
  SPREADSHEET_CELL     @map("spreadsheet_cell")
  PDF_TEXT_LAYER       @map("pdf_text_layer")
  PDF_OCR              @map("pdf_ocr")
  MANUAL_TRANSCRIPTION @map("manual_transcription")

  @@map("extraction_method")
}

/// What the source said about units for one extracted fact.
///
///   STATED         the source gave a unit
///   ABSENT         the source gave none
///   DIMENSIONLESS  the quantity has no unit (e.g. viscosity index)
///   UNRECOGNIZED   the source gave one this platform cannot yet interpret
enum SourceUnitClassification {
  STATED        @map("stated")
  ABSENT        @map("absent")
  DIMENSIONLESS @map("dimensionless")
  UNRECOGNIZED  @map("unrecognized")

  @@map("source_unit_classification")
}

/// How one SourceFact supports one normalized fact. SUPERSEDED evidence is
/// retained rather than unlinked — the audit trail must still show what an
/// earlier approval was based on.
enum EvidenceRole {
  PRIMARY       @map("primary")
  CORROBORATING @map("corroborating")
  SUPERSEDED    @map("superseded")

  @@map("evidence_role")
}

/// How confident a raw-property to SpecProperty mapping is. Recorded so medium
/// and low confidence mappings can be STORED without being treated as approved
/// domain truth — which is exactly what `reviewStatus` alongside it decides.
enum MappingConfidence {
  HIGH   @map("high")
  MEDIUM @map("medium")
  LOW    @map("low")

  @@map("mapping_confidence")
}

// --- Identity & Access -------------------------------------------------------

model User {
  id           String     @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  email        String     @unique
  passwordHash String     @map("password_hash")
  role         UserRole
  /// Whether this account may authenticate at all — ADR-012. NOT NULL with a
  /// database-level default, so a row written by any path (a raw INSERT
  /// included) is active unless it says otherwise, and no code path can
  /// create an account whose status is unknown. No endpoint writes it.
  status       UserStatus @default(ACTIVE)

  /// The credential cutoff: every access token and refresh session issued at
  /// or before this instant is permanently invalid (ADR-012 §7).
  ///
  /// NULL means no credential has ever been revoked for this account, which is
  /// the state every user is created in. It is set by the database — never by
  /// application code — when `status` goes active → disabled, and it is
  /// monotonic: re-enabling the account does NOT clear or lower it, which is
  /// what makes disable a revocation rather than a suspension.
  credentialsRevokedAt DateTime? @map("credentials_revoked_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  organizationId       String?   @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  createdAt            DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization                    Organization?              @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  authSessions                    AuthSession[]
  inquiriesSubmitted              Inquiry[]                  @relation("InquirySubmitter")
  inquiriesAssigned               Inquiry[]                  @relation("InquiryAssignee")
  formulationRequestsSubmitted    CustomFormulationRequest[] @relation("FormulationSubmitter")
  formulationRequestsAssigned     CustomFormulationRequest[] @relation("FormulationAssignee")
  distributorApplicationsAssigned DistributorApplication[]
  downloadRequestsAssigned        DownloadRequest[]
  blogPosts                       BlogPost[]
  statusChanges                   StatusHistory[]
  technicalReviews                TechnicalReview[]
  assignmentChangesMade           LeadAssignmentHistory[]    @relation("AssignmentHistoryActor")
  assignmentsHandedOver           LeadAssignmentHistory[]    @relation("AssignmentHistoryFrom")
  assignmentsReceived             LeadAssignmentHistory[]    @relation("AssignmentHistoryTo")

  @@index([organizationId])
  @@map("users")
}

/// One live refresh session — the server-side half of a login, and the only
/// thing that makes logout and revocation mean anything (ADR-012).
///
/// The raw refresh token is NEVER stored. tokenHash is its SHA-256 digest as
/// lowercase hex, which is what a lookup matches against; the raw value exists
/// only in the response to apps/web and in that tier’s own HttpOnly cookie. A
/// dump of this table therefore authenticates nobody.
///
/// Deliberately minimal. No ipAddress, userAgent, deviceName, lastUsedAt,
/// familyId or replacedById: this is authentication state, not a
/// device-management or analytics surface, and refresh-token family reuse
/// detection is explicitly deferred rather than half-modelled here.
model AuthSession {
  id        String    @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  userId    String    @map("user_id") @db.Uuid
  /// SHA-256 of the raw refresh token, lowercase hex, always 64 characters.
  /// Unique because a digest collision would mean two sessions answer to one
  /// token — the constraint is what makes "the session for this token" a
  /// question with at most one answer.
  tokenHash String    @unique @map("token_hash")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  expiresAt DateTime  @map("expires_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// Set once, by rotation or by logout, and never cleared. A revoked row is
  /// kept rather than deleted so a rotated token fails as revoked rather than
  /// as unknown. The client cannot tell the two apart — it sees one generic
  /// failure either way — but the row is the difference between a record and
  /// a hole.
  revokedAt DateTime? @map("revoked_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  user User @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The FK index PostgreSQL does not create on its own. It also serves the
  /// only non-token query shape that exists — this user’s sessions — which
  /// logout uses to scope revocation to the caller.
  @@index([userId])
  @@map("auth_sessions")
}

/// A customer's company. Internal staff have no organization; Customer users
/// belong to one — which is why User.organizationId is nullable.
model Organization {
  id        String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  name      String
  type      String?
  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  users User[]

  @@map("organizations")
}

// --- Catalog -----------------------------------------------------------------

model Category {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  name     String
  slug     String  @unique
  parentId String? @map("parent_id") @db.Uuid

  parent   Category?  @relation("CategoryHierarchy", fields: [parentId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  children Category[] @relation("CategoryHierarchy")
  products Product[]

  @@index([parentId])
  @@map("categories")
}

model Product {
  id   String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  name String
  slug String @unique

  /// The ratified catalog identity this Product was imported under, e.g.
  /// `SAMCAT-W1-R003`. INTERNAL: never served by a public DTO, the catalog API,
  /// the sitemap, web or the CMS — asserted by test, not by convention.
  ///
  /// Nullable because it is additive over rows that predate it and because a
  /// Product created outside the catalog import legitimately has none; a
  /// NOT NULL column would have made the migration impossible and would assert
  /// that every Product must come from a workbook, which is not true.
  ///
  /// Unique when present, so one ratified identity can name at most one row.
  /// Never derived from slug, name or worksheet row: after ratification the
  /// string is OPAQUE (ADR-011, PRODUCT-DATA-2C-A), and the database refuses to
  /// rewrite a non-NULL value to a different one.
  sourceRef String? @unique @map("source_ref") @db.VarChar(64)

  categoryId    String   @map("category_id") @db.Uuid
  /// The PRIMARY Product Type, single-valued in v2 (ADR-007 §4). Nullable in
  /// Phase 1 because no ProductType row is approved yet, and a required
  /// reference to an empty table would make Product un-insertable. Product ↔
  /// ProductType many-to-many is deferred, not omitted.
  productTypeId String?  @map("product_type_id") @db.Uuid
  description   String?
  createdAt     DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  category       Category         @relation(fields: [categoryId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  // Restrict rather than SetNull even though the column is nullable: silently
  // unclassifying every product of a deleted type is data loss, and `category`
  // — the other classification axis — restricts for the same reason.
  productType    ProductType?     @relation(fields: [productTypeId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  specifications Specification[]
  grades         ProductGrade[]
  claims         ProductClaim[]
  segments       ProductSegment[]
  inquiries      Inquiry[]        @relation("InquiryRelatedProduct")

  // No Media relation: product imagery and documents attach through Media's
  // polymorphic ownerType/ownerId, not a foreign key.

  @@index([categoryId])
  @@index([productTypeId])
  @@map("products")
}

/// One technical fact about a Product, or about ONE GRADE of a Product.
///
/// ── Two generations of columns live here, deliberately ──────────────────────
///
/// `key` / `value` are the ORIGINAL shape and are still what `GET /products/:slug`
/// serves today. They are NOT removed, renamed or rewritten by PRODUCT-DATA-2A:
/// every column below them is additive and nullable, so the table gains a
/// normalized, reviewable representation without the shipped one changing
/// meaning for a single existing reader. Removal is a later migration's decision
/// and needs its own evidence — see the migration header.
///
/// ── The grade axis, and why it is a COMPOSITE foreign key ───────────────────
///
/// `productGradeId` NULL  -> a Product-level fact (applies to the whole product)
/// `productGradeId` set   -> a Grade-level fact, belonging to a grade OF THE
///                           SAME PRODUCT
///
/// That second sentence is the invariant, and it is enforced by PostgreSQL, not
/// by a service. The relation references `(id, product_id)` on `product_grades`
/// rather than `id` alone, reusing this row's own `product_id` as the second
/// column. A grade belonging to a different Product therefore cannot be
/// referenced at all — the pair does not exist in the target key.
///
/// It works because a multi-column foreign key defaults to MATCH SIMPLE, which
/// skips the check entirely when ANY column is NULL. A Product-level fact
/// (`productGradeId` NULL) is thus unconstrained by it, and a Grade-level fact
/// is fully constrained by it. One mechanism, both cases, no trigger.
///
/// Measured against Prisma 7.9.1 and PostgreSQL 18 before it was adopted — the
/// composite reference validates, generates, and rejects a cross-product grade
/// at the database.
///
/// ── Deletion ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// `product` stays ON DELETE CASCADE — the shipped behaviour, unchanged. The
/// grade reference is RESTRICT: catalogue facts are retired with `deletedAt`,
/// never erased, and a grade that still carries facts is not deletable. Evidence
/// and review rows RESTRICT against this table in turn, so a Product delete that
/// would destroy an audit trail aborts rather than cascading through it.
///
/// ── ON UPDATE RESTRICT, and why it is not the default ───────────────────
///
/// Prisma emits ON UPDATE CASCADE unless told otherwise, and on a COMPOSITE key
/// that is actively dangerous: moving a grade to another product would cascade
/// the new product_id down into this row, silently re-parenting the fact to a
/// product it was never measured on. The invariant would hold at every instant
/// and the data would still be wrong.
///
/// Measured, not reasoned about — the first version of this migration used the
/// default, and a database probe moved a grade across products with its
/// specifications following it. RESTRICT is what makes that an error.
model Specification {
  id        String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  productId String @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  /// LEGACY. The original untyped key/value pair, still served by the catalog
  /// API. Kept NOT NULL and untouched; new normalized rows carry `propertyKey`
  /// and `displayValue` instead.
  key       String
  /// LEGACY — see `key`.
  value     String

  /// NULL for a Product-level fact; a grade OF THIS PRODUCT otherwise. The
  /// composite foreign key below is what makes "of this product" true.
  productGradeId String? @map("product_grade_id") @db.Uuid

  /// The controlled dictionary entry this fact is an instance of. Nullable
  /// because legacy rows predate the dictionary; required by CHECK as soon as
  /// `valueType` is set, so a normalized row is never uncontrolled.
  propertyKey String? @map("property_key")

  /// Exactly what a reader should see, as text — including any qualifier the
  /// numeric columns cannot carry. The numeric columns are for filtering and
  /// comparison; this is for display, and the two are never expected to be
  /// derivable from one another.
  displayValue String? @map("display_value")

  /// The shape of the value. NULL means this row has not been normalized yet.
  valueType SpecValueType? @map("value_type")

  /// The numeric payload, interpreted by `valueType` and constrained by the
  /// `specifications_value_shape` CHECK. Decimal rather than float: a
  /// specification limit that changes when it is round-tripped is not a limit.
  numericMin Decimal? @map("numeric_min") @db.Decimal(20, 6)
  numericMax Decimal? @map("numeric_max") @db.Decimal(20, 6)

  /// The two halves of a PAIR value — a coupled reading the source states
  /// together and which loses its meaning if split into two rows.
  pairFirst  Decimal? @map("pair_first") @db.Decimal(20, 6)
  pairSecond Decimal? @map("pair_second") @db.Decimal(20, 6)

  /// Already present before PRODUCT-DATA-2A and reused rather than duplicated.
  unit String?

  /// The test method the value was produced by, verbatim from the source.
  method String?

  /// A short source-stated qualification the numeric columns cannot express.
  /// Deliberately NOT a free-text review note: `TechnicalReview.note` is where
  /// reviewer prose belongs, and `valueQualifierNote` is not a column here.
  qualifier String?

  /// What the number IS. NOT NULL with an UNSPECIFIED default so that a row can
  /// never silently imply a specification limit it never claimed.
  resultBasis ResultBasis @default(UNSPECIFIED) @map("result_basis")

  /// The publication gate. NOT NULL, defaulting to SOURCE_RECORDED at the
  /// DATABASE — a row inserted by any path, a raw INSERT included, is
  /// unapproved unless a review says otherwise. `v_specification_public` shows
  /// APPROVED and nothing else.
  reviewStatus TechnicalReviewStatus @default(SOURCE_RECORDED) @map("review_status")

  sortOrder Int?      @map("sort_order")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// Soft deletion. Catalogue facts are retired, never erased, because a hard
  /// delete would take its evidence and its review history with it.
  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  product      Product                 @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  productGrade ProductGrade?           @relation(fields: [productGradeId, productId], references: [id, productId], onDelete: Restrict, onUpdate: Restrict)
  property     SpecProperty?           @relation(fields: [propertyKey], references: [key], onDelete: Restrict)
  evidence     SpecificationEvidence[]
  reviews      TechnicalReview[]

  // No unique on (productId, key) by decision: a product may carry the same key
  // more than once, e.g. one row per grade.

  @@index([productId])
  @@index([productGradeId])
  @@index([propertyKey])
  @@index([reviewStatus])
  @@map("specifications")
}

/// One grade of a Product — `SAE 10W-40`, `ISO VG 46`, `NLGI 2`.
///
/// ── What this is NOT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// No slug, no route, no localization, no SKU, and none may be added here. A
/// grade is not addressable: the canonical Product Detail URL is flat
/// (`/{locale}/products/{product-slug}`, ADR-009/ADR-010) and a grade is a
/// FACET WITHIN that page, not a page. It therefore takes no part in the
/// ADR-011 slug namespace — `product_slug_claims` is fed by `categories.slug`,
/// `products.slug` and `content_translations`, and this table is none of them.
///
/// `label` is the EXACT source label, stored verbatim. It is not parsed, not
/// normalized and not reformatted: the workbook's wording is the public wording.
///
/// `gradeSystem` NULL means not yet safely classified — a real state, not a gap
/// to be filled in by inference.
///
/// The 100 authoritative Products are NOT imported by this gate and no row here
/// is created by it. `Quenching` is not a Grade and `TC` is a performance
/// classification, not a Grade; neither may ever become a row. No synthetic or
/// default Grade exists — a Product with no grades has none, and its facts hang
/// off the Product directly.
model ProductGrade {
  id          String       @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  productId   String       @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  /// The exact label the source states. Verbatim.
  label       String
  /// NULL = not yet safely classified. See GradeSystem.
  gradeSystem GradeSystem? @map("grade_system")
  sortOrder   Int          @map("sort_order")
  createdAt   DateTime     @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  product        Product         @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict, onUpdate: Restrict)
  specifications Specification[]
  claims         ProductClaim[]

  /// One grade label per product, and the reason the importer can be re-run
  /// without producing duplicates.
  @@unique([productId, label])
  /// The COMPOSITE IDENTITY the invariant rests on. Redundant as a uniqueness
  /// statement — `id` is already the primary key — and required as a foreign-key
  /// TARGET: PostgreSQL will only reference a set of columns that carries a
  /// unique constraint, and `Specification`/`ProductClaim` reference
  /// `(id, product_id)` precisely so a grade cannot be borrowed across products.
  @@unique([id, productId])
  @@index([productId])
  @@map("product_grades")
}

/// The controlled dictionary of technical properties — INTERNAL, non-localized,
/// and not exposed publicly by this gate.
///
/// Its primary key is the stable key itself rather than a surrogate uuid: the
/// key IS the identity, it is what `Specification.propertyKey` stores, and a
/// dictionary whose entries can be renamed without anything noticing is not a
/// controlled vocabulary. `Specification` references it ON DELETE RESTRICT, so
/// an entry that is in use cannot be removed out from under the facts using it.
///
/// Seeded by nothing in this gate. Medium and low confidence mappings are
/// recorded in `SpecPropertyMapping` with their confidence and their own review
/// status — they must never be written here, because everything here is
/// approved domain truth by construction.
model SpecProperty {
  /// The stable key. Lowercase snake_case by convention; never renamed.
  key String @id

  /// What this property MEANS, in one sentence, for a reviewer. Not a public
  /// label and not localized — public wording lives in the translation catalogs.
  canonicalMeaning String @map("canonical_meaning")

  /// The physical quantity measured (e.g. `kinematic_viscosity`, `density`).
  /// Free text rather than an enum: no quantity vocabulary is approved, and an
  /// enum asserting one would be an unapproved architecture decision.
  quantity String

  valueKind SpecValueKind @map("value_kind")

  /// The units a value for this property may carry. Empty means none is valid —
  /// which is the correct state for a dimensionless or textual property.
  allowedUnits String[] @map("allowed_units")

  methodRequirement MethodRequirement @map("method_requirement")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  specifications Specification[]
  mappings       SpecPropertyMapping[]

  @@map("spec_properties")
}

/// A statement made ABOUT a Product or one of its Grades — a classification, a
/// suitability, an approval.
///
/// ── Why the kind is a closed type and not a boolean ─────────────────────────
///
/// "Formulated for" and "approved by" are legally different sentences. A bare
/// API/ACEA class the source merely states is CLASSIFICATION_STATED; an additive
/// target level is FORMULATED_FOR. Neither is an approval, and no code path may
/// promote one into another.
///
/// ── What the database refuses, regardless of application code ───────────────
///
/// Two CHECKs in the migration, not service rules:
///
///   * LICENSED_BY and REFERENCE_ONLY can NEVER reach APPROVED. The licensing
///     statements in the supplied external material are third-party claims this
///     platform has no right to republish, and reference-only exists precisely
///     to hold something that is never published — including the unnamed
///     automaker claim.
///   * APPROVED_BY can only reach APPROVED with a non-empty `standardBody`. An
///     approval by nobody in particular is not an approval. The second half of
///     that rule — that SAM verification is recorded — is a TechnicalReview row,
///     which is a cross-row fact a CHECK cannot see; it belongs to the review
///     service in the later gate.
model ProductClaim {
  id             String  @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  productId      String  @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  /// NULL = the claim applies to the whole Product. Otherwise a grade OF THIS
  /// PRODUCT, by the same composite foreign key `Specification` uses.
  productGradeId String? @map("product_grade_id") @db.Uuid

  kind ProductClaimKind

  /// The body named by the claim — `API`, `ACEA`, a manufacturer. NULL when the
  /// source names none, which is itself a reason the claim cannot be an
  /// approval.
  standardBody String? @map("standard_body")
  /// The specific class or specification code, verbatim.
  standardCode String? @map("standard_code")
  /// Source-stated context that changes what the claim means.
  contextNote  String? @map("context_note")

  reviewStatus TechnicalReviewStatus @default(SOURCE_RECORDED) @map("review_status")
  sortOrder    Int?                  @map("sort_order")
  createdAt    DateTime              @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  deletedAt    DateTime?             @map("deleted_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// Stable identity discriminator for an IMPORTED claim: the SHA-256 of the
  /// evidence identity of the source reading this claim was normalized from.
  ///
  /// It exists because the normalized columns cannot tell two real claims apart.
  /// Three products state two DIFFERENT suitabilities — "Suitable for manual
  /// gear box" and "Suitable for helical and spiral gear box" — which both
  /// normalize to `SUITABLE_FOR` with a NULL body, code and note. Without this
  /// column the two are one row and a genuine claim is lost; with it they are
  /// two rows and a replay can match each back to its own reading.
  ///
  /// It is an IDENTITY, not an evidence link. `ClaimEvidence` remains the only
  /// statement of which facts support a claim, and a claim may cite many.
  /// NULL for any claim not created by the importer.
  claimIdentityHash String? @map("claim_identity_hash") @db.Char(64)

  product      Product           @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  productGrade ProductGrade?     @relation(fields: [productGradeId, productId], references: [id, productId], onDelete: Restrict, onUpdate: Restrict)
  evidence     ClaimEvidence[]
  reviews      TechnicalReview[]

  @@index([productId])
  @@index([productGradeId])
  @@index([reviewStatus])
  @@map("product_claims")
}

/// A buyer-facing application/use axis — ADR-007 §4. ORTHOGONAL to Product
/// Family (`Category`), not a level inside it: one Segment spans several
/// Families, which is precisely what a child `Category` cannot do.
///
/// Deliberately minimal. No description, no isActive, no familyId and no
/// timestamps — `Category`, the axis this one sits beside, carries none of them
/// either. `sortOrder` exists because the approved set is a navigation list
/// whose order is editorial rather than alphabetical, the same reason `Locale`
/// carries one.
///
/// Nine approved Segment NAMES, eight persisted Segments: ADR-008 approves a
/// slug for eight of them and resolves `Other` as vocabulary rather than a row,
/// so a Product in no real Segment is represented by the ABSENCE of a
/// ProductSegment membership. Initial population is performed by the dedicated
/// catalog seed (prisma/seed-catalog.ts), which upserts by `slug` and never
/// deletes. Any future write mechanism requires its own approval.
model Segment {
  id        String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  name      String
  slug      String @unique
  sortOrder Int    @map("sort_order")

  products     ProductSegment[]
  productTypes SegmentProductType[]

  @@map("segments")
}

/// A reusable product classification, shared globally and NEVER duplicated per
/// Segment (ADR-007 §4): one row is visible from every Segment that uses it, so
/// a type can be renamed once and "which Segments use this type" stays
/// answerable.
///
/// No `sortOrder` here by decision — ordering is per-Segment and lives on
/// SegmentProductType, because a Segment publishes its own ordered subset.
model ProductType {
  id   String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  name String
  slug String @unique

  products Product[]
  segments SegmentProductType[]

  @@map("product_types")
}

/// Product ↔ Segment membership. Explicit rather than an implicit Prisma
/// many-to-many, for the same reason BlogPostTag is: the physical table has to
/// be named in snake_case like every other one.
///
/// Carries no payload — a product's segments have no defined order — which is
/// why DATA_MODEL.md §1 draws this as a direct many-to-many line rather than an
/// entity of its own. Cascade on both sides: a membership row is meaningless
/// without either of its ends.
model ProductSegment {
  productId String @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  segmentId String @map("segment_id") @db.Uuid

  product Product @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  segment Segment @relation(fields: [segmentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@id([productId, segmentId])
  @@index([segmentId])
  @@map("product_segments")
}

/// Which Product Types a Segment publishes, and in what order — ADR-007 §4.
///
/// `sortOrder` lives here and not on ProductType because a Segment publishes
/// its OWN ordered subset of the shared type set: the same type may sit at a
/// different position in two Segments, which one global column cannot express.
///
/// Deliberately NOT unique on (segmentId, sortOrder): such a constraint turns
/// every reorder into a multi-step update through a temporary offset. A stable
/// sequence is `sortOrder` then `productTypeId`.
///
/// No membership is approved — all nine per-Segment lists are pending, and none
/// may be inferred from the family sub-ranges.
model SegmentProductType {
  segmentId     String @map("segment_id") @db.Uuid
  productTypeId String @map("product_type_id") @db.Uuid
  sortOrder     Int    @map("sort_order")

  segment     Segment     @relation(fields: [segmentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  productType ProductType @relation(fields: [productTypeId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@id([segmentId, productTypeId])
  @@index([productTypeId])
  @@map("segment_product_types")
}

/// The `/{locale}/products/{slug}` namespace registry — [ADR-011](../docs/ADR/ADR-011-products-slug-namespace-enforcement.md).
///
/// One row per normalized slug key claimed by a Product Family (`Category`) or a
/// Product. `slugKey` is `lower(normalize(value, NFC))`, and **its primary key IS
/// the invariant**: ADR-010 §5's union spans three tables, and PostgreSQL has no
/// cross-table unique constraint, so the rule is expressed as a fourth relation
/// on which one unique key means what no per-table key can.
///
/// **TRIGGER-MAINTAINED. NEVER WRITE THIS TABLE FROM APPLICATION CODE** — not
/// from `apps/api`, not from a seed, not from an import. Nine statement-level
/// AFTER triggers on `categories`, `products` and `content_translations` claim
/// and release on its behalf, which is the whole point: every writer is enforced
/// without opting in, including the direct-Prisma seeds this repository is
/// currently made of. An application-written registry would silently drift from
/// the tables it describes, and a registry that can disagree with reality is
/// worse than none because it still looks authoritative.
///
/// One key belongs to exactly ONE entity globally, across every locale (INV-1) —
/// intentionally stricter than route disambiguation requires, for the reasons
/// ADR-011 §2 records. The SAME owner may back one key from several sources (its
/// base slug plus its translated slug rows) and still holds exactly ONE row here.
///
/// Deliberately absent, each because release RECOMPUTES from the source tables
/// rather than counting: no refcount, no source-row list, no `createdAt`, and no
/// surrogate id. A counter is a second copy of the truth that can drift; a
/// recomputed `EXISTS` cannot, and a hand-repaired row self-heals on next write.
model ProductSlugClaim {
  slugKey   String @id @map("slug_key")
  /// The literal claimed value, kept for debugging only — **never compared**.
  /// Comparison is always on `slugKey`. Where one owner holds the same key from
  /// several sources, this carries whichever the backfill or the claim saw.
  slug      String
  ownerType String @map("owner_type")
  ownerId   String @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid

  // No relation to Category or Product, and none is possible: `ownerType`
  // selects between two tables, the same polymorphic shape ContentTranslation
  // and SeoMeta already use. Owner existence is enforced in the trigger (INV-3),
  // scoped to these two entity types, not by a foreign key.

  @@index([ownerType, ownerId])
  @@map("product_slug_claims")
}

// --- Media -------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Polymorphic via ownerType/ownerId for owned assets (product imagery,
/// documents), and additionally referenced by foreign key where a specific
/// entity owns exactly one file (attachments, CVs, social images). Both
/// patterns coexist by design — see DATA_MODEL.md.
model Media {
  id        String    @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  url       String
  type      MediaType
  altText   String?   @map("alt_text")
  ownerType String?   @map("owner_type")
  ownerId   String?   @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid

  inquiryAttachments     Inquiry[]                  @relation("InquiryAttachment")
  formulationAttachments CustomFormulationRequest[] @relation("FormulationAttachment")
  jobApplicationCvs      JobApplication[]           @relation("JobApplicationCv")
  seoSocialImages        SeoMeta[]                  @relation("SeoSocialImage")

  @@index([ownerType, ownerId])
  @@index([type])
  @@map("media")
}

// --- Blog --------------------------------------------------------------------

model BlogPost {
  id          String    @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  title       String
  slug        String    @unique
  content     String
  categoryId  String    @map("category_id") @db.Uuid
  authorId    String?   @map("author_id") @db.Uuid
  publishedAt DateTime? @map("published_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  category BlogCategory  @relation(fields: [categoryId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  author   User?         @relation(fields: [authorId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  tags     BlogPostTag[]

  @@index([categoryId])
  @@index([authorId])
  @@index([publishedAt])
  @@map("blog_posts")
}

model BlogCategory {
  id   String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  name String
  slug String @unique

  posts BlogPost[]

  @@map("blog_categories")
}

model BlogTag {
  id   String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  name String
  slug String @unique

  posts BlogPostTag[]

  @@map("blog_tags")
}

/// Explicit join table rather than an implicit Prisma many-to-many, so the
/// physical table can be named in snake_case like every other table.
model BlogPostTag {
  blogPostId String @map("blog_post_id") @db.Uuid
  blogTagId  String @map("blog_tag_id") @db.Uuid

  blogPost BlogPost @relation(fields: [blogPostId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  blogTag  BlogTag  @relation(fields: [blogTagId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@id([blogPostId, blogTagId])
  @@index([blogTagId])
  @@map("blog_post_tags")
}

// --- Forms & Submissions -----------------------------------------------------

/// The single general-submission entity. Covers all six Contact Us inquiry
/// types plus Sample Request — there is no separate SampleRequest entity;
/// "Request Sample" CTAs submit an Inquiry with relatedProductId set.
model Inquiry {
  id                     String      @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  userId                 String?     @map("user_id") @db.Uuid
  assignedToId           String?     @map("assigned_to_id") @db.Uuid
  firstName              String      @map("first_name")
  lastName               String      @map("last_name")
  companyName            String      @map("company_name")
  country                String
  email                  String
  phone                  String?
  industry               String
  inquiryType            InquiryType @map("inquiry_type")
  productsOfInterest     String[]    @map("products_of_interest")
  relatedProductId       String?     @map("related_product_id") @db.Uuid
  requiredQuantity       String?     @map("required_quantity")
  destinationCountryPort String?     @map("destination_country_port")
  preferredIncoterm      String?     @map("preferred_incoterm")
  message                String?
  attachmentMediaId      String?     @map("attachment_media_id") @db.Uuid
  consentGiven           Boolean     @map("consent_given")
  /// The Privacy Policy revision this consent was recorded against — server-owned,
  /// written once on insert, and never re-derived afterwards. Its value is the
  /// constant ACTIVE_PRIVACY_POLICY_REVISION in apps/api (Forms module): no client
  /// supplies it, and it is not looked up in sam_cms, so a later CMS edit cannot
  /// silently rewrite what a past submitter agreed to.
  ///
  /// A plain human-readable revision identifier, never a Payload row or revision
  /// id and never a foreign key — sam_cms is a separate database (ADR-002) and
  /// Pages.lastUpdatedDate is an editor-set display field, not consent evidence
  /// (SECURITY.md #Personal Data Retention).
  ///
  /// NULL means no versioned Privacy Policy was in force when the consent was
  /// recorded. That is the literal truth of every row written today: no approved
  /// policy content exists, the consent labels link to nothing, and no value is
  /// invented to fill the column.
  ///
  /// **Immutable after INSERT, enforced by a BEFORE UPDATE trigger** that Prisma
  /// does not model (migration 20260818140000_privacy_policy_version_immutable):
  /// any update where the value IS DISTINCT FROM the stored one is rejected,
  /// NULL to a revision included. Every other column stays updateable.
  privacyPolicyVersion   String?     @map("privacy_policy_version")
  /// The lead's workflow state — one of exactly `new`, `in_progress`, `closed`.
  ///
  /// **Constrained by a database CHECK that Prisma does not model** (migration
  /// 20260820120000_add_lead_workflow): the column stays `String` rather than becoming a
  /// PostgreSQL enum, because a three-value vocabulary does not justify the cost of
  /// ALTER TYPE on every future change, and the CHECK gives the same integrity. Application
  /// validation exists as well, for message quality — the constraint is the authority.
  ///
  /// `new` = accepted and persisted, no work begun. `in_progress` = work has begun.
  /// `closed` = no further work intended, and **reopenable** to `in_progress`. Ownership is
  /// NOT a status: who holds the lead is `assignedToId`, which is why there is no
  /// `assigned` member here to disagree with it.
  ///
  /// Transitions are enforced in the Forms module and recorded in `StatusHistory`. The
  /// CHECK constrains the vocabulary, not the graph — a database constraint cannot see the
  /// previous value, so `closed → new` is refused by the service, not by Postgres.
  status                 String
  createdAt              DateTime    @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  user           User?    @relation("InquirySubmitter", fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  assignedTo     User?    @relation("InquiryAssignee", fields: [assignedToId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  relatedProduct Product? @relation("InquiryRelatedProduct", fields: [relatedProductId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  attachment     Media?   @relation("InquiryAttachment", fields: [attachmentMediaId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([status])
  @@index([assignedToId])
  @@index([inquiryType])
  @@index([createdAt])
  @@index([userId])
  @@index([relatedProductId])
  @@index([attachmentMediaId])
  @@map("inquiries")
}

model CustomFormulationRequest {
  id                     String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  userId                 String?  @map("user_id") @db.Uuid
  assignedToId           String?  @map("assigned_to_id") @db.Uuid
  companyName            String   @map("company_name")
  country                String
  industry               String
  email                  String
  phone                  String?
  productOrApplication   String   @map("product_or_application")
  requiredSpecifications String   @map("required_specifications")
  estimatedQuantity      String?  @map("estimated_quantity")
  packagingRequirements  String?  @map("packaging_requirements")
  additionalInformation  String?  @map("additional_information")
  destinationCountry     String?  @map("destination_country")
  preferredIncoterm      String?  @map("preferred_incoterm")
  attachmentMediaId      String?  @map("attachment_media_id") @db.Uuid
  consentGiven           Boolean  @map("consent_given")
  /// The Privacy Policy revision this consent was recorded against — server-owned,
  /// written once on insert, and never re-derived afterwards. Its value is the
  /// constant ACTIVE_PRIVACY_POLICY_REVISION in apps/api (Forms module): no client
  /// supplies it, and it is not looked up in sam_cms, so a later CMS edit cannot
  /// silently rewrite what a past submitter agreed to.
  ///
  /// A plain human-readable revision identifier, never a Payload row or revision
  /// id and never a foreign key — sam_cms is a separate database (ADR-002) and
  /// Pages.lastUpdatedDate is an editor-set display field, not consent evidence
  /// (SECURITY.md #Personal Data Retention).
  ///
  /// NULL means no versioned Privacy Policy was in force when the consent was
  /// recorded. That is the literal truth of every row written today: no approved
  /// policy content exists, the consent labels link to nothing, and no value is
  /// invented to fill the column.
  ///
  /// **Immutable after INSERT, enforced by a BEFORE UPDATE trigger** that Prisma
  /// does not model (migration 20260818140000_privacy_policy_version_immutable):
  /// any update where the value IS DISTINCT FROM the stored one is rejected,
  /// NULL to a revision included. Every other column stays updateable.
  privacyPolicyVersion   String?  @map("privacy_policy_version")
  /// The lead's workflow state — one of exactly `new`, `in_progress`, `closed`.
  ///
  /// **Constrained by a database CHECK that Prisma does not model** (migration
  /// 20260820120000_add_lead_workflow): the column stays `String` rather than becoming a
  /// PostgreSQL enum, because a three-value vocabulary does not justify the cost of
  /// ALTER TYPE on every future change, and the CHECK gives the same integrity. Application
  /// validation exists as well, for message quality — the constraint is the authority.
  ///
  /// `new` = accepted and persisted, no work begun. `in_progress` = work has begun.
  /// `closed` = no further work intended, and **reopenable** to `in_progress`. Ownership is
  /// NOT a status: who holds the lead is `assignedToId`, which is why there is no
  /// `assigned` member here to disagree with it.
  ///
  /// Transitions are enforced in the Forms module and recorded in `StatusHistory`. The
  /// CHECK constrains the vocabulary, not the graph — a database constraint cannot see the
  /// previous value, so `closed → new` is refused by the service, not by Postgres.
  status                 String
  createdAt              DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  user       User?  @relation("FormulationSubmitter", fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  assignedTo User?  @relation("FormulationAssignee", fields: [assignedToId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  attachment Media? @relation("FormulationAttachment", fields: [attachmentMediaId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([status])
  @@index([assignedToId])
  @@index([createdAt])
  @@index([userId])
  @@index([attachmentMediaId])
  @@map("custom_formulation_requests")
}

model DistributorApplication {
  id                         String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  assignedToId               String?  @map("assigned_to_id") @db.Uuid
  companyName                String   @map("company_name")
  contactPerson              String   @map("contact_person")
  countryTerritory           String   @map("country_territory")
  email                      String
  phone                      String?
  website                    String?
  yearsInBusiness            String?  @map("years_in_business")
  currentProductLines        String?  @map("current_product_lines")
  sectorsServed              String?  @map("sectors_served")
  estimatedAnnualVolume      String?  @map("estimated_annual_volume")
  storageCapacity            String?  @map("storage_capacity")
  brandsCurrentlyDistributed String?  @map("brands_currently_distributed")
  additionalInformation      String?  @map("additional_information")
  consentGiven               Boolean  @map("consent_given")
  status                     String
  createdAt                  DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  assignedTo User? @relation(fields: [assignedToId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([status])
  @@index([assignedToId])
  @@index([createdAt])
  @@map("distributor_applications")
}

/// Admin-only. Deliberately carries NO assignedToId: a job application is not a
/// sales lead and must never enter a Sales Expert's queue (SECURITY.md). Do not
/// "fix" this inconsistency with the other submission entities — it is the
/// schema-level counterpart of the RBAC carve-out.
model JobApplication {
  id            String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  firstName     String   @map("first_name")
  lastName      String   @map("last_name")
  email         String
  phone         String?
  /// Soft key to Payload's JobOpenings collection in sam_cms — never a foreign
  /// key (ADR-002), resolved by NestJS. Null means a speculative application.
  jobOpeningKey String?  @map("job_opening_key")
  coverLetter   String?  @map("cover_letter")
  cvMediaId     String?  @map("cv_media_id") @db.Uuid
  consentGiven  Boolean  @map("consent_given")
  status        String
  createdAt     DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  cv Media? @relation("JobApplicationCv", fields: [cvMediaId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([status])
  @@index([createdAt])
  @@index([jobOpeningKey])
  @@index([cvMediaId])
  @@map("job_applications")
}

/// Gates the Company Catalogue and Product Catalogue only. TDS and SDS
/// downloads are deliberately ungated.
model DownloadRequest {
  id           String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  assignedToId String?  @map("assigned_to_id") @db.Uuid
  name         String
  companyName  String   @map("company_name")
  country      String
  email        String
  documentType String   @map("document_type")
  /// Soft key: a catalogue may be a Payload upload or a Prisma Media record, so
  /// this spans the ADR-002 split and cannot be a foreign key.
  documentKey  String   @map("document_key")
  consentGiven Boolean  @map("consent_given")
  createdAt    DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  // No status field in DATA_MODEL.md, and correspondingly no StatusHistory.

  assignedTo User? @relation(fields: [assignedToId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([assignedToId])
  @@index([createdAt])
  @@index([email])
  @@map("download_requests")
}

/// Standalone by design — a subscriber is not an account holder, so there is no
/// relation to User. status/confirmedAt support double opt-in.
model NewsletterSubscription {
  id             String           @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  email          String           @unique
  locale         String
  status         NewsletterStatus @default(PENDING)
  source         String
  consentGiven   Boolean          @map("consent_given")
  confirmedAt    DateTime?        @map("confirmed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  unsubscribedAt DateTime?        @map("unsubscribed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  createdAt      DateTime         @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  localeRef Locale @relation(fields: [locale], references: [code], onDelete: Restrict, onUpdate: Cascade)

  @@index([status])
  @@index([createdAt])
  @@index([locale])
  @@map("newsletter_subscriptions")
}

// --- SEO ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Polymorphic (entityType + entityId) so any content type can carry SEO
/// fields. One record per entity per locale. Slug lives on the owning entity,
/// never here. docs/seo/SEO_ARCHITECTURE.md is authoritative on field meaning.
model SeoMeta {
  id                     String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  entityType             String   @map("entity_type")
  entityId               String   @map("entity_id") @db.Uuid
  locale                 String
  metaTitle              String?  @map("meta_title")
  metaDescription        String?  @map("meta_description")
  canonicalUrl           String?  @map("canonical_url")
  ogTitle                String?  @map("og_title")
  ogDescription          String?  @map("og_description")
  ogImageUrl             String?  @map("og_image_url")
  twitterCardType        String?  @map("twitter_card_type")
  twitterTitle           String?  @map("twitter_title")
  twitterDescription     String?  @map("twitter_description")
  twitterImageUrl        String?  @map("twitter_image_url")
  robotsIndex            Boolean  @default(true) @map("robots_index")
  robotsFollow           Boolean  @default(true) @map("robots_follow")
  keywords               String[]
  structuredDataOverride Json?    @map("structured_data_override")
  socialImageId          String?  @map("social_image_id") @db.Uuid

  localeRef   Locale @relation(fields: [locale], references: [code], onDelete: Restrict, onUpdate: Cascade)
  socialImage Media? @relation("SeoSocialImage", fields: [socialImageId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@unique([entityType, entityId, locale])
  @@index([entityType, entityId])
  @@index([socialImageId])
  @@index([locale])
  @@map("seo_meta")
}

/// Flat and non-polymorphic: it operates on paths, not records, so a redirect
/// survives deletion of whatever entity once lived at that path.
model Redirect {
  id         String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  fromPath   String   @map("from_path")
  toPath     String   @map("to_path")
  statusCode Int      @default(301) @map("status_code")
  locale     String?
  isActive   Boolean  @default(true) @map("is_active")
  createdAt  DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  localeRef Locale? @relation(fields: [locale], references: [code], onDelete: Restrict, onUpdate: Cascade)

  // Note: this unique does NOT constrain rows where locale IS NULL, because
  // PostgreSQL treats NULLs as distinct. The redirects_from_path_global partial
  // index in the first migration closes that gap.
  @@unique([fromPath, locale])
  @@index([fromPath, isActive])
  @@index([locale])
  @@map("redirects")
}

// --- Internationalization ----------------------------------------------------

/// The single source of truth for which languages are active. Adding a language
/// is a row here plus translated content — never a code change.
model Locale {
  id         String          @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  code       String          @unique
  name       String
  nativeName String          @map("native_name")
  direction  LocaleDirection
  isActive   Boolean         @default(true) @map("is_active")
  isDefault  Boolean         @default(false) @map("is_default")
  sortOrder  Int             @map("sort_order")

  seoMetaRecords          SeoMeta[]
  contentTranslations     ContentTranslation[]
  redirects               Redirect[]
  newsletterSubscriptions NewsletterSubscription[]

  @@index([isActive, sortOrder])
  @@map("locales")
}

/// Generic per-field translation for Prisma-owned content (Product, Category,
/// BlogPost). The base entity's own field holds the default locale's value;
/// every other locale is a row here.
model ContentTranslation {
  id                String            @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  entityType        String            @map("entity_type")
  entityId          String            @map("entity_id") @db.Uuid
  locale            String
  field             String
  value             String
  translationStatus TranslationStatus @map("translation_status")

  localeRef Locale @relation(fields: [locale], references: [code], onDelete: Restrict, onUpdate: Cascade)

  @@unique([entityType, entityId, locale, field])
  @@index([entityType, entityId])
  @@index([locale, translationStatus])
  @@map("content_translations")
}

// --- Audit -------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Generic, polymorphic status-change audit trail. Attaches to Inquiry,
/// CustomFormulationRequest, DistributorApplication, JobApplication and
/// ContentTranslation by entityType/entityId — no foreign keys, because one
/// table cannot hold an FK to five different parents. The anchor point the
/// future Workflow module extends.
model StatusHistory {
  id                     String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  entityType             String   @map("entity_type")
  entityId               String   @map("entity_id") @db.Uuid
  fromStatus             String?  @map("from_status")
  toStatus               String   @map("to_status")
  changedById            String?  @map("changed_by_id") @db.Uuid
  /// Who acted, as text, captured at write time — and never updated afterwards.
  ///
  /// `changedById` is `ON DELETE SET NULL`, and deleting a `User` row is this platform's
  /// strongest credential revocation (ADR-012 §7) rather than an exceptional event. Without
  /// this column, off-boarding a member of staff silently anonymises every audit row they
  /// ever wrote — the trail survives and stops naming anyone. Measured, not theorised: a
  /// DELETE against a user with history leaves the row in place with a NULL actor.
  ///
  /// So the FK keeps the live link while it exists, and this keeps the record true after it
  /// does not. Email is the whole snapshot: it is already the human-readable operator
  /// identity everywhere in this application, and no password, token, role or profile field
  /// belongs in an audit row.
  ///
  /// Nullable because rows written before this column existed have nothing to record, and
  /// because a future system actor may have no email at all.
  changedByEmailSnapshot String?  @map("changed_by_email_snapshot")
  changedAt              DateTime @default(now()) @map("changed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  note                   String?

  changedBy User? @relation(fields: [changedById], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([entityType, entityId])
  @@index([changedById])
  @@index([changedAt])
  @@map("status_history")
}

/// Who owned a lead, and who changed that — the assignment counterpart to `StatusHistory`.
///
/// ── Why this is a separate table and not more columns on `StatusHistory` ────
///
/// `StatusHistory.toStatus` is NOT NULL, so an assignment-only row cannot be written there
/// without making a shipped column nullable — on a **polymorphic table `ContentTranslation`
/// also uses**. Widening it would push lead-specific assignee columns onto a translation
/// audit trail and change the meaning of a table two unrelated features depend on. One
/// narrow table is the smaller change, and it leaves `StatusHistory` exactly as documented.
///
/// ── Polymorphic in the same shape, and for the same reason ──────────────────
///
/// `entityType`/`entityId` with no foreign key to the parent, matching `StatusHistory`,
/// `SeoMeta` and `Specification`: one table cannot hold an FK to two different parents.
/// `entityType` is `Inquiry` or `CustomFormulationRequest` and nothing else — the two lead
/// kinds with an assignment surface. `DistributorApplication` and `DownloadRequest` carry
/// `assignedToId` too and will reuse this table when they get endpoints; no schema change
/// will be needed for them.
///
/// ── Both ends of the handover are snapshotted ───────────────────────────────
///
/// An A → B reassignment must stay readable when either A or B is later deleted, so the
/// previous owner, the new owner and the actor are each recorded as an id **and** as text.
/// See `StatusHistory.changedByEmailSnapshot` for why the FK alone is not enough.
///
/// A cleared assignment is `toAssigneeId = NULL` with a NULL snapshot — the absence is the
/// record, and there is no sentinel value for it.
model LeadAssignmentHistory {
  id         String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  entityType String @map("entity_type")
  entityId   String @map("entity_id") @db.Uuid

  /// The owner before this change. NULL means the lead was unassigned.
  fromAssigneeId            String? @map("from_assignee_id") @db.Uuid
  fromAssigneeEmailSnapshot String? @map("from_assignee_email_snapshot")

  /// The owner after this change. NULL means the assignment was cleared.
  toAssigneeId            String? @map("to_assignee_id") @db.Uuid
  toAssigneeEmailSnapshot String? @map("to_assignee_email_snapshot")

  /// The Admin who performed the change. Only Admin may assign.
  changedById            String? @map("changed_by_id") @db.Uuid
  changedByEmailSnapshot String? @map("changed_by_email_snapshot")

  changedAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("changed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  note      String?

  fromAssignee User? @relation("AssignmentHistoryFrom", fields: [fromAssigneeId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  toAssignee   User? @relation("AssignmentHistoryTo", fields: [toAssigneeId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  changedBy    User? @relation("AssignmentHistoryActor", fields: [changedById], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([entityType, entityId])
  @@index([changedById])
  @@index([changedAt])
  @@map("lead_assignment_history")
}

// --- Technical Data Provenance (PRODUCT-DATA-2A) ------------------------------
//
// IMPORT AND AUDIT ONLY. Nothing in this section may ever appear in a public
// DTO, a public view, a sitemap, an SEO payload or a RAG index. External sources
// and provenance are categorically non-public — not "non-public by default", and
// not "non-public unless flagged": there is no publishable flag anywhere in this
// section, and `SourceDocument.isPublishable` is absent on purpose so that no
// caller can ever ask the question.
//
// The two public views (`v_specification_public`, `v_product_claim_public`,
// created in the migration) select from `specifications` and `product_claims`
// only. No view, and no future view, joins any table below.
//
// TDS bytes and external images are never stored here and never imported.
// `SourceAsset` records a file's IDENTITY — a hash and a size — not its content.

/// The identity of a source file, without the file.
///
/// `sha256` is the whole point: it is what makes "the same document" and "a new
/// revision of that document" distinguishable, and it is unique, so one physical
/// file is one row no matter how many documents cite it.
///
/// NO BYTES. This platform does not store TDS content, and adding a column that
/// could hold it would defeat the decision rather than implement it.
model SourceAsset {
  id        String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  /// Lowercase hex SHA-256 of the file, always 64 characters.
  sha256    String @unique
  byteSize  Int    @map("byte_size")
  mediaType String @map("media_type")
  /// Pages, where the medium has pages. NULL for a spreadsheet.
  pageCount Int?   @map("page_count")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  documents SourceDocument[]

  @@map("source_assets")
}

/// A cited source — a supplier TDS, a standards page, or the authoritative
/// catalog workbook this project was given.
///
/// ── Locator, not URL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// The workbook has no URL and never will, so a required `url` column would have
/// made the project's own primary source uncitable. `locatorType` +
/// `locatorValue` addresses both cases with one shape: a URL, or a file name.
///
/// ── Revisions ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// The same locator may legitimately be recorded more than once — a supplier
/// republishes a TDS at the same address with different content. That is a new
/// revision, a new `SourceAsset` hash, and a new row here; `supersededBy` links
/// the old row forward. The uniqueness key is therefore
/// (locatorType, locatorValue, sourceAssetId), NOT the locator alone.
///
/// `sourceAssetId` is nullable because a source can be cited before, or without,
/// a captured file — a standards web page read in a browser has no asset. Note
/// that PostgreSQL treats NULLs as distinct in a unique index, so rows with no
/// asset are not de-duplicated by it; that is accepted rather than worked
/// around, because two citations of an uncaptured page are genuinely two
/// citations.
model SourceDocument {
  id String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid

  sourceAssetId String?           @map("source_asset_id") @db.Uuid
  locatorType   SourceLocatorType @map("locator_type")
  /// The URL, or the file name of the uploaded document. Never fetched again by
  /// this gate.
  locatorValue  String            @map("locator_value")

  publisher     String?
  title         String
  documentDate  DateTime? @map("document_date") @db.Date
  revisionLabel String?   @map("revision_label")
  retrievedAt   DateTime  @map("retrieved_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// What numbers in THIS document are, absent a per-fact override. A supplier
  /// TDS that says "typical values" sets it once here rather than on every fact.
  defaultResultBasis ResultBasis @default(UNSPECIFIED) @map("default_result_basis")

  /// The later revision that replaces this one. RESTRICT: a superseding document
  /// cannot be deleted while it is still the forward pointer of an older one.
  supersededById String? @map("superseded_by_id") @db.Uuid

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  sourceAsset  SourceAsset?     @relation(fields: [sourceAssetId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  supersededBy SourceDocument?  @relation("SourceDocumentSupersession", fields: [supersededById], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  supersedes   SourceDocument[] @relation("SourceDocumentSupersession")
  importRuns   ImportRun[]
  facts        SourceFact[]

  @@unique([locatorType, locatorValue, sourceAssetId])
  @@index([sourceAssetId])
  @@index([supersededById])
  @@map("source_documents")
}

/// One execution of an importer. Every SourceFact names the run that produced
/// it, so a bad extraction is identifiable and reversible as a set rather than
/// row by row.
///
/// `sourceDocumentId` is nullable: a run over several documents belongs to none
/// of them in particular.
model ImportRun {
  id               String  @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  sourceDocumentId String? @map("source_document_id") @db.Uuid

  /// What produced the facts, precisely enough to reproduce them.
  importerVersion String    @map("importer_version")
  startedAt       DateTime  @default(now()) @map("started_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  finishedAt      DateTime? @map("finished_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  note            String?

  /// The canonical manifest hash this run applied. Unique among FINISHED runs,
  /// so the same plan cannot be applied twice and be recorded as two successes.
  /// An abandoned or failed run keeps `finishedAt` NULL and does not consume the
  /// hash, so a retry after a rollback is still allowed. NULL for a run that
  /// applied no manifest.
  manifestHash String? @map("manifest_hash") @db.Char(64)

  sourceDocument SourceDocument? @relation(fields: [sourceDocumentId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  facts          SourceFact[]

  @@index([sourceDocumentId])
  @@map("import_runs")
}

/// One raw fact exactly as the source stated it — IMMUTABLE after insert,
/// enforced by a BEFORE UPDATE trigger in the migration, not by convention.
///
/// This is the bottom of the evidence chain. A normalized `Specification` can be
/// corrected; what the source SAID cannot, because a rewritable record of what a
/// document claimed is not evidence of anything. Corrections are new rows plus a
/// SUPERSEDED evidence role, never an UPDATE.
///
/// Every `raw*` column is verbatim text, deliberately unparsed: interpretation
/// happens on the way to `Specification`, and keeping the original means a
/// mis-interpretation is always recoverable.
model SourceFact {
  id               String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  sourceDocumentId String @map("source_document_id") @db.Uuid
  importRunId      String @map("import_run_id") @db.Uuid

  /// Where in the document. Whichever of these the medium has.
  pageNumber  Int?    @map("page_number")
  sheetName   String? @map("sheet_name")
  rowNumber   Int?    @map("row_number")
  columnLabel String? @map("column_label")

  /// Verbatim, unparsed source text.
  rawProperty String? @map("raw_property")
  rawUnit     String? @map("raw_unit")
  rawValue    String  @map("raw_value")
  rawMethod   String? @map("raw_method")
  rawGrade    String? @map("raw_grade")

  extractionMethod   ExtractionMethod         @map("extraction_method")
  unitClassification SourceUnitClassification @map("unit_classification")

  /// Overrides the document's `defaultResultBasis` for this fact alone, when the
  /// source says something different in one place.
  resultBasisOverride ResultBasis? @map("result_basis_override")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  sourceDocument     SourceDocument          @relation(fields: [sourceDocumentId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  importRun          ImportRun               @relation(fields: [importRunId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  specificationLinks SpecificationEvidence[]
  claimLinks         ClaimEvidence[]

  @@index([sourceDocumentId])
  @@index([importRunId])
  @@map("source_facts")
}

/// Which SourceFacts support which Specification — many-to-many, in both
/// directions, because both directions genuinely occur: two documents can
/// corroborate one normalized value, and one workbook cell can be the evidence
/// for a value AND for the grade it belongs to.
///
/// Both foreign keys RESTRICT. Deleting a Specification or a SourceFact that
/// participates in an evidence link is refused outright — this row IS the audit
/// history, and a cascade here would erase the reason an approval was given.
model SpecificationEvidence {
  specificationId String       @map("specification_id") @db.Uuid
  sourceFactId    String       @map("source_fact_id") @db.Uuid
  role            EvidenceRole
  note            String?
  createdAt       DateTime     @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  specification Specification @relation(fields: [specificationId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  sourceFact    SourceFact    @relation(fields: [sourceFactId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  @@id([specificationId, sourceFactId])
  @@index([sourceFactId])
  @@map("specification_evidence")
}

/// The ProductClaim counterpart of SpecificationEvidence, with the same
/// cardinality and the same RESTRICT rules.
model ClaimEvidence {
  productClaimId String       @map("product_claim_id") @db.Uuid
  sourceFactId   String       @map("source_fact_id") @db.Uuid
  role           EvidenceRole
  note           String?
  createdAt      DateTime     @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  productClaim ProductClaim @relation(fields: [productClaimId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  sourceFact   SourceFact   @relation(fields: [sourceFactId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  @@id([productClaimId, sourceFactId])
  @@index([sourceFactId])
  @@map("claim_evidence")
}

/// A proposed correspondence between a raw source property string and a
/// `SpecProperty` key.
///
/// This table exists so that uncertain mappings have somewhere to live that is
/// NOT the dictionary. `confidence` records how good the guess is and
/// `reviewStatus` records whether anyone has agreed with it; a MEDIUM or LOW row
/// sitting here at SOURCE_RECORDED is explicitly not approved domain truth, and
/// nothing may read it as such.
///
/// `specPropertyKey` is nullable: "we saw this property and have no mapping for
/// it" is a result worth recording, and the unresolved list the importer must
/// report is exactly the set of rows where it is NULL.
model SpecPropertyMapping {
  id String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid

  /// The source's own wording, verbatim.
  rawProperty String  @map("raw_property")
  /// The source's own unit, verbatim, where the same wording maps differently
  /// depending on it.
  rawUnit     String? @map("raw_unit")

  specPropertyKey String? @map("spec_property_key")

  confidence   MappingConfidence
  reviewStatus TechnicalReviewStatus @default(SOURCE_RECORDED) @map("review_status")
  note         String?
  createdAt    DateTime              @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  specProperty SpecProperty? @relation(fields: [specPropertyKey], references: [key], onDelete: Restrict)

  @@unique([rawProperty, rawUnit])
  @@index([specPropertyKey])
  @@index([reviewStatus])
  @@map("spec_property_mappings")
}

/// One recorded SAM technical review decision — the only thing that can make a
/// technical fact public.
///
/// ── Exactly one subject ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// A review is OF a Specification or OF a ProductClaim, never both and never
/// neither. Enforced by the `technical_reviews_exactly_one_target` CHECK, not by
/// a service: a review with two subjects, or none, is not a review.
///
/// Both subject foreign keys RESTRICT, so a reviewed row cannot be deleted out
/// from under its own review history.
///
/// ── The evidence-set hash, and why approval expires ─────────────────────────
///
/// `evidenceSetHash` is the fingerprint of exactly what the reviewer looked at.
/// It is computed by the database functions the migration installs
/// (`specification_evidence_set_hash`, `product_claim_evidence_set_hash`), and
/// its definition is deterministic and fixed:
///
///   1. take every evidence link for the subject
///   2. for each, the SourceFact id and the SHA-256 of the SourceAsset behind
///      that fact's SourceDocument (empty string where no asset was captured)
///   3. render one line per link as `<sourceFactId>:<sha256>`
///   4. sort those lines by byte value, ascending — so the hash does not depend
///      on insertion order
///   5. join with newline, encode UTF-8, SHA-256, lowercase hex
///
/// Recomputing it later and finding a different value means the evidence changed
/// after the approval, and the approval therefore no longer describes the facts
/// in front of it. ENFORCING that — refusing to serve, or resetting the status —
/// is review-service behaviour and belongs to the later API/admin gate. This
/// gate installs the schema and the functions that make it computable.
///
/// ── Reviewer identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// `reviewerId` is ON DELETE SET NULL, matching `StatusHistory.changedById`, and
/// `reviewerEmailSnapshot` is NOT NULL. ADR-012 makes deleting a User this
/// platform's strongest credential revocation, so a RESTRICT here would make an
/// approved specification block an off-boarding. The snapshot is what keeps the
/// record true afterwards: the FK goes, the name does not.
model TechnicalReview {
  id String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid

  /// Exactly one of these two is set. See the CHECK.
  specificationId String? @map("specification_id") @db.Uuid
  productClaimId  String? @map("product_claim_id") @db.Uuid

  reviewerId            String? @map("reviewer_id") @db.Uuid
  /// Who decided, captured at write time and never updated. NOT NULL: a review
  /// that names nobody is not accountable, and deleting the user must not be
  /// able to produce one.
  reviewerEmailSnapshot String  @map("reviewer_email_snapshot")

  reviewedAt DateTime                @default(now()) @map("reviewed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  decision   TechnicalReviewDecision
  note       String?

  /// The deterministic fingerprint of the reviewed evidence set — 64 lowercase
  /// hex characters. See the model comment for the exact definition.
  evidenceSetHash String @map("evidence_set_hash")

  specification Specification? @relation(fields: [specificationId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  productClaim  ProductClaim?  @relation(fields: [productClaimId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  reviewer      User?          @relation(fields: [reviewerId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([specificationId])
  @@index([productClaimId])
  @@index([reviewerId])
  @@index([reviewedAt])
  @@map("technical_reviews")
}
